\par
[Coach]: Welcome! Just to be clear from the start, this is a coaching session, not a test. It is perfectly okay\f1\emdash and even encouraged\emdash to say 'I don't know' or 'I made a mistake.' That's the fastest way we'll learn today. To get us started and set up our workspace, could you please confirm your name?\par
\par
[Sampson]: Sampson Longjohn\par
\par
[Coach]: Thanks, Sampson! Let's dive right in. To kick things off with our first topic, Handling Form Submissions, could you paste a non-trivial code snippet showing how you extract data from a request body and pass it along to your application's functions?\par
\par
[Sampson]: \par
const registerCategory = async (req, res, next) => \{\par
    // 1. Extract data safely from the incoming HTTP request body object\par
    const \{ category_name \} = req.body;\par
    \par
    // 2. Fetch the results of any validation middleware run prior to the controller\par
    const errors = validationResult(req);\par
\par
    // 3. Conditional block checking if any field broke server rules\par
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) \{\par
        return res.render('categories/add-category', \{\par
            title: 'Add New Category',\par
            errors: errors.array(),\par
            category_name // Sent back to sticky-populate the form view field\par
        \});\par
    \}\par
\par
    try \{\par
        // 4. Pass the extracted data down to the application's model level execution layer\par
        const result = await insertCategory(category_name);\par
        \par
        if (result) \{\par
            req.flash('notice', `The $\{category_name\} category was successfully added.`);\par
            res.redirect('/categories');\par
        \} else \{\par
            req.flash('notice', 'Sorry, adding the category failed.');\par
            res.status(501).render('categories/add-category', \{\par
                title: 'Add New Category',\par
                errors: null,\par
                category_name\par
            \});\par
        \}\par
    \} catch (error) \{\par
        next(error); // Forwards database execution errors into the global centralized app error handler\par
    \}\par
\};\par
\par
[Coach]: This is a solid, clean implementation of a controller function, Sampson. Looking at your code, what is the specific reason or advantage of passing category_name back to the res.render call inside your validation error block (if (!errors.isEmpty()))?\par
\par
[Sampson]: When server-side validation fails, the application re-renders the registration page to show the error list. Without passing the variable back, the form input field clears completely, forcing the user to type the name all over again. By sending the data back, the EJS value attribute (value="<%= category_name %>") repopulates the input field with their exact text, preserving their input for a seamless user experience.\par
\par
[Coach]: Spot on, Sampson. That "sticky" form behavior saves users a lot of frustration. Let's move on to our second topic, Forms for Updates. Could you paste a non-trivial EJS snippet showing how you insert existing data into a form so a user can edit an entity?\par
\par
[Sampson]: \par
<main>\par
