        next(error);\par
    \}\par
\};\par
\par
[Coach]: Excellent configuration and setup, Sampson. Using custom middleware to automatically map req.flash('notice') to res.locals.notice is an elegant way to make flash messages universally accessible across your views. Based on how flash messages work under the hood, what would happen to that 'notice' message if you refreshed the /categories page immediately after the redirection completed, and why does that happen?\par
\par
[Sampson]: Under the hood, connect-flash stores data in a temporary array inside the session object (req.session.flash). When the application reads the flash message to display it (which happens automatically on the first page load via the middleware req.flash('notice')), the package immediately deletes that key from the session memory.\par
\par
[Coach]: Perfect explanation. The data is cleared instantly upon being read from the session. If you refresh the page, the message disappears entirely because it has already been consumed. Let's move to our fourth and final topic, Server-side Validation. Could you provide a relevant code snippet showing your approach to validating user input on the server side?\par
\par
[Sampson]: \par
// 1. Defined in src/utilities/category-validation.js\par
import \{ body \} from 'express-validator';\par
\par
export const categoryRules = () => \{\par
    return [\par
        // Validates the 'category_name' input field from the form\par
        body('category_name')\par
            .trim()\par
            .escape()\par
            .notEmpty().withMessage('Please provide a category name.')\par
            .isLength(\{ min: 3 \}).withMessage('Category name must be at least 3 characters long.')\par
            .isLength(\{ max: 100 \}).withMessage('Category name cannot be longer than 100 characters.')\par
    ];\par
\};\par
\par
// 2. Applied in src/routes.js\par
router.post('/new-category', categoryRules(), registerCategory);\par
\par
// 3. Handled in src/controllers/categories.js\par
import \{ validationResult \} from 'express-validator';\par
\par
const registerCategory = async (req, res, next) => \{\par
    const \{ category_name \} = req.body;\par
    \par
    // Extracts validation errors gathered by the middleware from the request object\par
    const errors = validationResult(req);\par
\par
    // Evaluates if any validation rules were violated\par
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) \{\par
        return res.render('categories/add-category', \{\par
            title: 'Add New Category',\par
            errors: errors.array(), // Converts errors to an array to be looped over in EJS\par
            category_name\par
        \});\par
    \}\par
    \par
    // Proceed to database insertion if validation passes...\par
\};\par
\par
[Coach]: This is an excellent implementation of express-validator across your layers, Sampson. In your rules, you chain .trim() and .escape() right before checking if the field is empty or matching its length. Why is it critical to sanitize the input with methods like .trim() and .escape() before running validations like .notEmpty() or checking the length, rather than doing it afterward?\par
\par
[Sampson]: It is critical to sanitize the input before running validations because sanitization directly alters the characters and length of the data being checked. Running these steps in the correct order prevents bypasses and ensures accuracy. If we validate first: The string "   " passes the .notEmpty() check because its length is 3. The application proceeds with invalid data. If we sanitize first: The .trim() method strips out all whitespace characters, reducing "   " to an empty string "". When .notEmpty() runs immediately after, it catches the empty string and correctly flags the validation error. The .escape() method converts dangerous HTML special characters into secure HTML entities to prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. This process significantly expands the character count of the string.\par
\par
[Coach]: Spot on, Sampson! Your explanation of how .trim() reduces whitespace to an empty string to catch bypasses is exactly why order matters. We have successfully covered all four required topics. You did a fantastic job explaining your design choices and technical logic today.\par
